From: gcw_6a3w2yXz <gcw_6a3w2yXz@example.com>
Subject: Define portable __WORDSIZE fallback for non-glibc libcs
Origin: local portability fix for HarmonyOS PC (HongMeng libc++ / musl-like,
 no <bits/wordsize.h>); __WORDSIZE is a glibc-internal macro and the
 upstream `#include <bits/user.h>` fallback does not define it on OHOS,
 breaking alignment/statistics/StatUtils.cpp with
 "use of undeclared identifier '__WORDSIZE'" (true-machine preflight
 [81/186] StatUtils.cpp:18). The fallback keeps glibc shift semantics
 (31 on 64-bit, 15 on 32-bit); no algorithm change.

--- a/alignment/statistics/StatUtils.cpp
+++ b/alignment/statistics/StatUtils.cpp
@@ -1,5 +1,11 @@
 #include <alignment/statistics/StatUtils.hpp>
 
 #ifndef __WORDSIZE
-#include <bits/user.h>
+// __WORDSIZE is a glibc-internal macro (<bits/wordsize.h>); non-glibc
+// libcs (OHOS/musl with libc++) provide neither it nor a defining
+// <bits/user.h>. Fall back to the long/pointer width so the
+// (__WORDSIZE / 2 - 1) shift below keeps its glibc semantics
+// (31 on 64-bit, 15 on 32-bit). sizeof is evaluated by the compiler,
+// not the preprocessor, so this is valid in the shift expression.
+#define __WORDSIZE (sizeof(unsigned long) * 8)
 #endif
